PDF import into HST Draw: transparent text keeps its opacity, and text keeps its place in the
drawing order.

A watermark line drawn with a transparent graphics state (/ca 0.1), such as the per-download
name that document systems add, came in as opaque black text: the text style wrote only the
colour. It now also writes loext:opacity, which the import maps to the character transparency;
the document root declares the loext namespace for it.

A text line becomes a frame only when the next glyph starts another line or the page ends, and
it took its z-order at that moment. The last text of a page (often the watermark) therefore went
on top of the images and paths drawn after it, for example the stamp images of signature
widgets. The line now takes its z-order when its first glyph is drawn. When an image, path or
group is drawn while a line is pending, the next glyph starts a new line, so the rest of the line
lies above it: a highlight drawn under a word in the middle of a line stays below that word.

A transparency group (a form XObject) painted with a constant alpha came in opaque: poppler sets the
fill opacity to 1 inside the group and applies the group's alpha only in paintTransparencyGroup,
which the import ignored. HST's own PDF export writes a translucent watermark exactly like that
("q /ca 0.1 gs /Fm Do Q"), so re-opening an exported or signed PDF in Draw showed it solid. The
poppler output device now reports paintTransparencyGroup with the fill opacity, and the alpha is
multiplied into everything drawn in the group just ended (its pending text is flushed into the
group first).

Tests: a watermark line followed by a rectangle (opacity and order), a highlight under a word in
the middle of a line, and text in a transparency group painted with /ca 0.1.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).

